{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Elleh</b> — the sense of <b>vaheḥarashti</b>: because I am slow to anger, and it is as though I was silent." "<b>Damiti</b> — as though I do not know hidden things as you do; therefore after it: <b>ochikha</b> and <b>ʿarekha leʿeinekha</b>, like <b>ʿarekha lefanekha</b>. And the meaning: for your mother's son did not know what you spoke in secret. And Rabbi Moses said: it is impossible to say that it is <b>shamar</b> in the perfect or <b>shmor</b> with shva under the shin in the imperfect, but rather with kamatz gadol, and likewise <b>ʿasot ʾeʿeseh</b>. Now what does one do with the word <b>heyot</b> — <b>ehyeh kamokha</b>, and there is no other form besides it."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {1 "Rabbi Moses's grammatical argument is compressed and elliptical; rendered hyper-literally. He objects that damiti cannot derive from a normal shamar conjugation, and raises heyot as a further difficulty, since its form ahyeh does not fit the pattern he describes."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:31:35.365161686Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}